{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:f3494ccab12eb673d29279a1acec4add62d3523657b8f111f36f480797fd433a","modelForecastIds":["sha256:9e32442dd80104ef31ec692efe35319d2d9fb5d07be418ab3e540ee18caa519a"],"modelUse":"consulted","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.6},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.6}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PUBLISHED ADAPTER (rule 1). Entry point /Users/adam/github/overlapping-generations/.claude/worktrees/demographic-ai-cross-impact-52e65e/scripts/tetlock-sim-adapter.ts. Translation it performs: CQ3 = (GDP_2036/GDP_2031)^(1/5) - 1 over summed regional GDP for us + oecd-ex-us + china. Model readings, 10 scenarios: baseline 2.946, central-path 2.945, ai-energy-boom 3.031, tech-breakthrough 3.682, automation-boom 2.999, ssp3-70 2.617, regional-divergence 2.500, tech-plateau 2.291, tech-stagnation 2.299, debt-populism 1.674. Only debt-populism (1.674) reaches my P50 neighbourhood. I ADOPT NONE of these.\n\nWHY THE MODEL DISAGREES (four identified reasons): (1) its China leg runs 2.85-4.58%/yr and does NOT implement the locked -2.7 pp/yr China aging drag — its demographics module carries fertility convergence, not a pension/property/dissaving adjustment; (2) its production function is Ayres-Warr biophysical with gamma=0.55 on useful energy, so China's electricity buildout mechanically manufactures GDP (the adapter's world 2036 datacenter load of 1,884-4,180 TWh exceeds the locked AI-Q3 P90 of 1,574 TWh even though world must exceed OECD-38+China, which is an independent sign the energy-to-output channel is hot); (3) the adapter's own COVERAGE GAP disclosure: us + oecd-ex-us is an OECD-core proxy that places Korea, Japan and Turkiye in seasia/mena and Mexico/Chile/Colombia/Costa Rica in latam, so it is not the OECD-38 aggregate; (4) LOW-FERTILITY-CONDITIONAL: CLAUDE.md and the adapter both record that the model tracks the UN WPP LOW variant (~18% below medium by 2100), so every bound read off it is conditional on low fertility — which for a 2032-36 window biases labour force DOWN only mildly (births after 2025 do not enter the workforce before ~2045) but means the model's high growth is not coming from a generous demographic path, i.e. it is coming from energy and TFP. USE: I take only one thing from the model — its PPP weight split (OECD-core proxy 0.629 / China 0.371 at 2031), as a cross-check on my own weights.\n\nMY WEIGHTS, RECONCILED: real OECD-38 is larger than the model's proxy. OECD-38 is ~45% of world PPP GDP, China ~19.5%, so within the basket OECD-38 = 45/64.5 = 0.698 and China = 0.302 in 2025; China's share creeps up on faster growth, so I use w_OECD = 0.68, w_China = 0.32 for 2032-36. That is ~5 pp more OECD weight than the model's proxy, which mechanically LOWERS the basket because OECD grows slower.\n\nTHE REQUIRED CHINA ARITHMETIC: w_China x locked China aging drag = 0.32 x (-2.7 pp) = -0.86 pp/yr of the basket. OECD leg: 0.68 x (-0.4 pp, the locked 1.0% -> 0.6% per-capita cut) = -0.27 pp/yr. TOTAL LOCKED AGING DRAG ON THE BASKET = -1.136 pp/yr. Independent consistency check: the IMF locked figure is \"aging slows GLOBAL growth by ~-1.1 pp/yr\". My bottom-up basket drag of -1.14 pp reproduces it. That is the single most load-bearing number in this answer.\n\nOECD-38 GROWTH ACCOUNTING, 2032-36 P50:\n+0.60 locked per-capita baseline (post-aging-drag, embeds 2015-22 trend TFP)\n+0.20 population growth (migration-driven; US ~0.3, EU ~0.05, Japan/Korea negative)\n= 0.80 headline baseline absent productivity offsets\n+0.31 expected AI TFP lift, computed from AI-Q6's OWN regime probabilities rather than asserted: 0.162x0.75 + 0.472x0.35 + 0.365x0.06 = 0.1215 + 0.1652 + 0.0219 = 0.3086 pp/yr\n-0.05 coupling haircut on measured TFP: the demographic pull directs AI into health, care, education and public administration, where output is measured by input, so diffusion registers as spending not as TFP; plus the fiscal constraint on adoption subsidy\n+0.10 capital deepening, net. Build: AI capex ~$350B/yr OECD plus grid ~$400B/yr against OECD GFCF of ~$15.4T (22% of ~$70T PPP) = ~5% of GFCF = +1.1 pp of GDP on the investment rate. With alpha=0.33 and K/Y=3, dK/K rises 1.1/3 = 0.37 pp, contributing 0.33x0.37 = +0.12 pp/yr. Haircut for AI-Q4's 66% impairment prior → +0.10. REQUIRED ASSUMPTIONS, stated per my lens brief: fleet utilization 70-85%; power+cooling operating cost 25-35% of TCO; take-or-pay contract duration 3-7 yr; refinancing at my CQ4 P50 real yield; residual value of 5-yr-old accelerators 10-20% of cost; discount rate = 8-10% real WACC. Critically, GPU D&A at 4-6 years means the NET capital stock contribution goes to ~0 once the build plateaus, which it does inside this window.\n+0.15 healthy-aging participation offset (the locked +0.4 pp/yr is a GLOBAL 2025-2050 figure acting mainly through extended working lives; the OECD-38 slice inside a 5-year window, net of what the 0.6% per-capita figure already prices, is small)\n-0.15 frictions: climate damages ~-0.05 (model 2036 temperature 1.715-1.739C, so this is genuinely small in-window); energy cost / interconnect-queue drag -0.05; fiscal interest reallocation -0.05 (net interest 2%->3% of GDP over ~6 years = 0.17 pp of GDP/yr at a ~0.3 multiplier differential)\n= 1.21%/yr. I use 1.2.\n\nCHINA 2032-36 P50: labour -1.1 pp (headcount: 60M over 2026-35 is -0.42%/yr total population, working-age -1.0 to -1.3%/yr; worker/retiree 4.4->2.8) + capital deepening +2.2 pp (I/Y ~40% but falling, and the property stock's marginal product near zero so effective deepening is below the raw rate) + TFP +1.5 pp (industrial policy, EV/battery/solar frontier, and roughly half the world's industrial robot installs) + human capital +0.3 pp - 0.5 (property adjustment, LGFV deleveraging) - 0.2 (pension/fiscal burden) = 2.2%/yr. CROSS-CHECK against the locked drag: a no-aging China counterfactual of ~4.9% minus 2.7 = 2.2%. The two routes agree. I allow 2.4 for the part of the 4.8%->2.2% fall that is pure convergence and might be double-counted in the -2.7.\n\nBASKET P50 = 0.68x1.2 + 0.32x2.4 = 0.816 + 0.768 = 1.58 → 1.6%/yr.\nLEVEL SANITY: the basket runs ~2.70%/yr today (0.68x1.7 + 0.32x4.8 = 1.16+1.54). My forecast of 1.6% is a -1.1 pp decline, which IS the locked -1.1 pp/yr aging effect. Internally consistent with 2A's own arithmetic.\nP10 = 0.9: coupled bust — AI-Q4's 66% realizes, capital deepening -> 0, TFP lift -> +0.05, BIS Box C demand deficit, China property/pension spiral, OECD fiscal retrenchment at high real rates. 0.68x0.65 + 0.32x1.2 = 0.44 + 0.38 = 0.83.\nP90 = 2.6: Transformative Breakout (16.2%) amplified by the labour-scarcity demand pull, healthy-aging participation surprise, China avoiding the property drag via an industrial-policy export surge. 0.68x2.0 + 0.32x3.8 = 1.36 + 1.22 = 2.58.\nMy P90 (2.6) sits BELOW the model's baseline (2.95) and far below tech-breakthrough (3.68). That gap is the payload, not an error.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.501Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE three-year average for 2027-2029, as published in OECD Main Economic Indicators / Economic Outlook. My build says ~2.9%/yr. Falsifier: the realized 2027-2029 average comes in BELOW 2.5%/yr.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My front-loaded inflation path is wrong and the relative-price skew is resolving on the deflating leg far earlier than I model. CQ2 falls from 24 to ~12 (conjunct A's marginal drops 0.55 -> 0.30). CQ6: move ~8 points from C to D (C 24 -> 16, D 22 -> 30), B unchanged. CQ4 P50 falls ~30 bps to ~1.8%. Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 55 to ~70.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD long-term-care and health workforce headcount growth versus real LTC wage growth, 2027-2030, from OECD Health at a Glance plus national LTC staffing registers. My claim is rationing-not-wages. Falsifier: LTC worker headcount grows faster than 3.5%/yr while real LTC wages grow under 1%/yr (elastic supply, no shadow price).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The care-labour Baumol wall is not binding and the fiscal-rationing channel is wrong. CQ1 conjunct B falls from 0.20 to ~0.10, taking CQ1 from 15 to ~8. CQ3 P50 rises ~0.15 pp to ~1.75 (care is supplied without a growth-sapping wage transfer). CQ6: ~5 points from C to B. Hypothesis plank (a) rises from 8 to ~25.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Real industrial/commercial electricity prices in the constrained interconnection nodes (PJM, Dublin/Ireland, Northern Virginia, eastern-China provincial grids), 2029-2031 average versus 2026, from EIA Electric Power Monthly, Eurostat nrg_pc_205 and SEMO/EirGrid. My claim is that dispatchable grid capacity and interconnection queue time is the binding weak link through ~2031. Falsifier: the 2029-2031 real average is NOT at least 20% above its 2026 level in any two of these four nodes.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The grid shadow price never materialized, so the 2027-2031 inflationary impulse in my path is overstated and the weak link is elsewhere (most likely care labour alone). CQ6-C falls from 24 to ~16 and D rises from 22 to ~28. CQ2 falls to ~17. My AI-Q3 coupling note weakens: a cheap, unconstrained grid makes the locked AI-Q3 P50 of 998 TWh more reachable, so my stated tension with AI-Q4 sharpens rather than resolves.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI, three-year average over 2031-2033, from the National Bureau of Statistics. My split-window build has 2031-2036 at ~1.9%/yr and the 10-year average at 1.46%. Falsifier: the 2031-2033 average exceeds 2.0%/yr.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2034\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The labour-scarcity services-wage channel has beaten the property-adjustment and household-income-share channels earlier than I model, and the deliberate supply-side overcapacity has been absorbed. CQ2 conjunct B collapses from 0.48 to ~0.15, taking CQ2 from 24 to ~9. CQ3 China leg rises ~0.4 pp, lifting the basket P50 from 1.6 to ~1.75. CQ6: ~4 points from D to B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Hyperscaler announced-versus-delivered AI capex gap and net external AI software/API revenue relative to AI compute D&A, from 10-K/20-F segment disclosures of the five largest buyers. AI-Q4 (66%) says the gap or the revenue shortfall shows up. Falsifier: the cumulative announced-vs-delivered capex gap stays under 10% through FY2031 AND net external AI revenue exceeds 1.0x compute D&A in every year of 2029-2031.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"AI-Q4 is wrong, the locked AI-Q3 P50 of 998 TWh is reachable, and the coupling tension I report resolves in favour of AI-Q3. My capital-deepening term rises from +0.10 to +0.20 pp/yr and my expected TFP lift from +0.31 to ~+0.45 pp/yr, taking CQ3 P50 from 1.6 to ~2.0 and P90 to ~3.0. CQ6-A rises from 9 to ~17 with B absorbing the rest; CQ6-D falls from 22 to ~14. CQ5 conjunct B falls from 0.45 to ~0.30 (the mechanical -25% equity drawdown driver is removed), taking CQ5 from 31 to ~21. CQ4 P50 rises ~25 bps to ~2.35%.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 fermi seat, effort 'inflation'."}}